munity he grew up with  in london we're in the middle of a  global pandemic  um it's been pretty tough for a lot of  chinese restaurants family restaurants  how are you feeling at this moment  you know initially back in march there  was this kind of the stigma  of of the chinese community um  and there was this india reaction you  know if you looked at  my friends in chinatown for example  their restaurants were completely  empty right but the the glimmer of hope  that i saw was that when restaurants  reopened  actually people went back to chinese  restaurants very very quickly  but i do think that nowadays chinese  cuisine  and chinese culture is an important part  of british culture  for wong and his family food is at the  heart of that  you've said this before this is your  dad's restaurant it's now yours  it's doing phenomenally well uh  what do you think your dad would have  made of all of this um  that's that's a hard one um  because he would have been proud of what  we've achieved  but no but he would not have been proud  for the journey it took to get here  and because he much would have preferred  me to be a doctor um  even still with two michelin stars i  think so i have the sauce for the potato  for cbs this morning saturday infio's  tied london  two michelin stars aside though i i i'm  telling you  i cannot wait to dive into that next  trip right i love it  let's do it hey wong next time we go to  london there i'll tell you what though  that's a hardcore dad right two michelin  stars and he's not to wouldn't be  wouldn't be thrilled with that it's  pretty amazing when you're the child of  immigrants like that though because  doctor is a known career path michelin  stars are not commonplace  yeah yeah i have to tell you that voice  of his love it

